## Sedans and pickup trucks were the most common vehicle types involved in fatal crashes on both weekdays and weekends. The overall distribution of vehicle types was similar across the two groups, although weekdays had a higher number of vehicles involved in fatal crashes.

### Test 1: Are fatal crashes more common on weekends than weekdays (per day)?

```{r}
# Chi-square test: weekend vs weekday counts vs expected 5/7, 2/7
weekend_counts <- accident_weekend |>
  count(weekend)

chisq.test(weekend_counts$n, p = c(5/7, 2/7))

```

Interpretation χ² = 281.66, df = 1, p \< 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶

This tests whether fatal crashes are distributed across weekend/weekday in proportion to the number of days each has (5/7 weekday, 2/7 weekend) — the "no difference in daily rate" null.

With p this small, you reject the null hypothesis: fatal crashes are not occurring at a constant rate per day across the week. Combined with your earlier EDA (weekends showed a higher average number of crashes per day than weekdays), this confirms that gap is statistically real, not just noise from your sample.

Suggested write-up line: "A chi-square goodness-of-fit test comparing observed weekend/weekday fatal crash counts to the expected 2/7–5/7 split by number of days showed a statistically significant difference (χ² = 281.66, df = 1, p \< .001), indicating that fatal crashes occur at a higher rate per day on weekends than on weekdays."

### Test 2: Does vehicle type distribution differ between weekend and weekday crashes?

```{r}
# Chi-square test: vehicle type distribution by weekend/weekday
vehicle_weekend_table <- vehicle_plot |>
  filter(vehicle_type %in% top_vehicle) |>
  count(weekend, vehicle_type) |>
  pivot_wider(names_from = vehicle_type, values_from = n, values_fill = 0) |>
  column_to_rownames("weekend") |>
  as.matrix()

chisq.test(vehicle_weekend_table)

```

Interpretation χ² = 380.95, df = 5, p \< 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶

This tests whether vehicle type distribution is independent of weekend vs. weekday (df = 5 because you compared 6 vehicle types across 2 day-type groups: (6-1) × (2-1) = 5).

p this small means you reject the null of independence — the mix of vehicle types involved in fatal crashes genuinely differs between weekends and weekdays, not just by chance.

Suggested write-up line: "A chi-square test of independence between vehicle type and weekend/weekday status was significant (χ² = 380.95, df = 5, p \< .001), indicating that the distribution of vehicle types involved in fatal crashes differs between weekends and weekdays."

